  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reducing and managing Human-Andean Bear conflict in Peru</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Andean Bear (Tremarctos ornatus) is the only ursid found in South America, threatened by habitat fragmentation and conflict with humans, and categorized as vulnerable to extinction. Human-Andean Bear conflicts (HBCs) arise when farmers experience a loss with their crops or livestock leading to the automatic assumption that it is due to Andean Bears, often triggering retaliatory (illegal) hunting of this protected species. Andean Bears are mainly herbivores so livestock predation is unusual at best, and improved crop production methods can mitigate crop damage. This project will support conservation efforts for the Andean Bear in Peru by strengthening the capacity and knowledge of key stakeholders (Ministries of Environment and Agriculture and locally affected farmers) by developing effective measures to mitigate HBCs at a national level and applying them in local pilot areas. The Peruvian government has an Andean Bear Strategy but the implementation and monitoring of this strategy requires support. Specific project activities focus on improving the Peruvian governments capacity to coordinate responses to HBCs on a national level and reducing HBC impact in at least three pilot sites through improved, alternative production techniques that support Andean Bear protection.</narrative>
  </description>
